      "title": "Anthropic tells AI inquiry it ‘never tried to dictate’ Australian copyright rules",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2026/oct/06/openai-australia-parliament-inquiry-jason-kwon",
      "description": "<p>OpenAI acknowledges ‘more work to do to rebuild trust with the Australian people’ as ABC says copyright laws do not need changing</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Anthropic has told a parliamentary inquiry on AI that it is not seeking to dictate copyright law on model training, but the current rules make it “impossible” to train AI in Australia.</p><p>OpenAI has used an appearance before the parliamentary committee to apologise again to Australia about the hack on Medicare last month, on the same day media and entertainment organisations argue weakening Australian copyright law for AI model training would leave artists as “roadkill”.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/media/2026/oct/06/openai-australia-parliament-inquiry-jason-kwon\">Continue reading...</a>",
